  "answer": "I can't give you an AUD-to-peso rate from my data, because the Reserve Bank of Australia doesn't publish one. Its exchange rate table covers about 25 currencies, and the Argentine peso isn't one of them.\n\n**What I can give you:** on **16 October 2026** (the latest RBA figure, at 4pm Sydney time), **A$1 = US$0.6947**. It has stayed between about US$0.693 and US$0.700 so far in October.\n\n**How to work out the peso rate:** multiply that figure by the current number of pesos per US dollar.\n\n  **Pesos per A$ ≈ 0.6947 × (pesos per US$)**\n\nFor example, if US$1 bought 1,000 pesos, A$1 would buy about 695 pesos.\n\n**Where to find the dollar-to-peso rate:** check Argentina's central bank (BCRA) or a live currency site such as XE or Google Finance. Argentina has had more than one exchange rate. Besides the official rate there are parallel ones, such as the \"blue\" (informal) rate and the \"MEP\" (bond-market) rate, and they can differ a lot. The official rate is the one to use for most purposes. If you're travelling, what you actually get will depend on whether you pay by card or swap cash.",
